  "description": "<p>Given the arrival and departure times (in minutes after midnight) of <code>n</code> trains for one day, find the <b>minimum number of platforms</b> so that no train waits. A train occupies its platform from its arrival minute to its departure minute <b>inclusive</b>: if one train arrives at the same minute another departs, they need different platforms. Train <code>i</code>'s departure is never earlier than its arrival.</p><ul><li>1 &le; n &le; 2000, 0 &le; arrival &le; departure &le; 1439</li></ul><h4>Input</h4><p>Line 1: <code>n</code>. Line 2: <code>n</code> arrival times. Line 3: <code>n</code> departure times (the <code>i</code>-th departure belongs to the <code>i</code>-th arrival).</p><h4>Output</h4><p>The minimum number of platforms.</p><h4>Example</h4><p>Input:</p><pre>6\n900 940 950 1100 1430 1000\n910 1200 1120 1130 1439 1000</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>3</pre>",
  "solution": "n = int(input())\narr = sorted(map(int, input().split()))\ndep = sorted(map(int, input().split()))\ni = j = cur = best = 0\nwhile i < n:\n    if arr[i] <= dep[j]:\n        cur += 1\n        i += 1\n        best = max(best, cur)\n    else:\n        cur -= 1\n        j += 1\nprint(best)\n",
